{"entries":{"chapter-38-study-benign-prostate-disorders|Chapter 38 Study: Benign Prostate Disorders":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|BPH is histologic hyperplasia; benign enlargement, bladder outlet obstruction and lower urinary tract symptoms overlap only partly (p555).":["u3"],"copy|LUTS include storage and voiding symptoms and can reflect comorbidity, medications or functional impairment rather than prostate disease alone (p555).":["u4"],"copy|IPSS/AUASI scores 0–7 are mild, 8–19 moderate and 20–35 severe; symptom bother and quality of life also guide decisions (p559).":["u5"],"copy|Examine cognition, mobility, neurologic function, prostate/rectal findings and stool impaction; rectal examination is inaccurate for prostate volume (p561).":["u6"],"copy|Urinalysis is routine; PSA testing follows appropriate cancer-screening decisions rather than automatically accompanying LUTS (p561).":["u7"],"copy|Urodynamics is not routine initially. High bladder pressure with low flow supports obstruction; consider testing after inadequate response or before surgery in selected frail patients (p562).":["u8"],"copy|Address reversible contributors and use shared decisions; many patients can begin with education, behavioral measures or watchful waiting (p563, p566).":["u9"],"copy|Tamsulosin 0.4–0.8 mg daily or alfuzosin 10 mg daily can improve LUTS; dizziness and hypotension still occur despite greater selectivity (p566).":["u10"],"copy|Tamsulosin can cause ejaculatory effects and floppy iris syndrome. Alert the cataract surgeon; stopping it does not reliably eliminate the risk (p566).":["u11"],"copy|Finasteride/dutasteride shrink the prostate and reduce progression risk, but symptom improvement can take 6–10 months; sexual adverse effects are possible (p566–567).":["u12"],"copy|These reductase inhibitors lower PSA by 40–60% during the first year, complicating interpretation where screening is appropriate (p567).":["u13"],"copy|Tadalafil 5 mg daily is approved for male LUTS in the chapter; combining PDE inhibitors with alpha blockers can cause dangerous hypotension (p566–567).":["u14"],"copy|Add-on antimuscarinic or mirabegron benefit is modest; weigh antimuscarinic harms, blood-pressure effects and limited evidence in patients with large residuals (p567).":["u15"],"copy|Alpha blockers treat symptoms rapidly relative to reductase inhibitors, but the latter have evidence for reducing retention/surgery risk (p566–567).":["u16"],"copy|Prostate size, obstruction and symptom severity correlate weakly; a high symptom score does not itself diagnose obstruction (p555, p562).":["u17"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u18"],"copy|2024-May Q68":["u19"],"copy|CHAPTER 38s / HAZZARD":["u20"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 38 - Benign Prostate Disorders; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u21"],"copy|Definitions":["u22"],"copy|Assessment":["u23"],"copy|Management & drugs":["u24"],"copy|Exam traps":["u25"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (1):":["u26"]},"maxId":26}
